{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791033771,"nonce":"9Soiw/a0cU7I+Cb8LHwaq73XbFE=","title":"EReg.replace and split are quadratic: every match converts the whole subject","message":"`hlp_regexp_match` (std/src/regexp.rs) scanned the UTF-16 subject for its terminator, copied it, converted all of it to UTF-8, and walked it from the start, character by character, to map each offset. EReg's replace, split and map call it once per match at an advancing position, so their cost grew with the square of the subject's length.\n\nMac, `--mode hybrid`, ~200 KB subject (regexbench.hl, this session's scratchpad):\n- one `~/[0-9]+/g.replace`: 6,596 ms\n- one `~/[,;\\s]+/g.split`: 12,979 ms\n- five replaces on a 188,890-unit string: 77.8 s\n\nFix: the regex keeps its last subject converted, with a table from each UTF-16 unit index to its UTF-8 byte, recognised on the next call by address plus one memcmp of its units. After: 91 ms, 167 ms, 1.0 s, identical results.\n\nThe memcmp is still linear per call. Recognising a GC string by address alone would make it free, since the cache sits in the scanned regexp block and keeps the buffer alive, but that needs a way to ask whether an address is inside a live GC allocation; `is_gc_ptr` answers from the last collection's marks and is false for anything allocated since.","files":null}]}